{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Rule 6c-11 does **not** require an AP to be a clearing-agency member or to clear/settle directly. It requires the ETF to have a written agreement with each AP allowing creation/redemption transactions; the AP must be a participant in a clearing agency registered under Exchange Act §17A, or have a written agreement with such a participant that permits it to clear and settle ETF transactions.\n\nThat is the rule’s eligibility test—not a requirement that the AP itself be a bank or a direct NSCC/DTC member.\n\nIn the bank’s reviewed U.S. AP agreement forms, the named contracting parties are typically the ETF/trust and the AP. A clearing broker or other clearing-agency participant may be involved under a separate agreement or arrangement; it is not automatically a party to the ETF–AP agreement. The forms’ party structure should not be confused with Rule 6c-11’s broader permitted-counterparty language."}}
